Proposed BUILD-DISCIPLINE pattern: an event stream that only fires after manual intervention measures the OPERATOR, not the machine -- and reads as resilience. From senechal 2026-07-28. Proposal only; senechal did not edit doctrine unilaterally.

THE CASE. Zach's Bluetooth mouse dropped repeatedly. The journal showed 15 'BLUETOOTH HID ... Mouse' attach events on 2026-07-27, each with an incrementing HID instance number. Read at a glance -- and this is how it was nearly read -- that looks like a link that recovers fifteen times, i.e. a system with working resilience and a flaky peripheral. It was the exact opposite. The adapter could not reconnect at all; every one of those fifteen events was Zach noticing, giving up, and toggling Bluetooth or re-pairing by hand. The log was a record of the human, rendered in the same syntax as a record of the machine.

WHY IT GENERALISES BEYOND BLUETOOTH. Any signal emitted at recovery, rather than at failure, has this property when recovery is manual: retry-succeeded lines where the retry is a human re-running the job, 'service started' with no matching 'service failed', reconnect/re-auth events, queue-drained metrics where someone drains the queue. The denominator -- how often a person had to intervene -- is invisible, so the numerator looks like health. The more diligent the operator, the healthier the system appears, which inverts the signal exactly when it matters.

THE DISCIPLINE ASK, one line: a metric counted from recovery events is only a health metric if recovery is known to be automatic. Otherwise it is an unlabelled measure of operator effort. Where the two cannot be told apart in the log, say so next to the threshold rather than in a comment somewhere else.

WHAT SENECHAL DID WITH IT, as a reference implementation rather than a claim: remedies/bt-mouse-link-stability.sh counts those re-attaches as its 'link stability witness' and carries the caveat inline, in both the threshold's config comment (senechal.json.example health._comment_bluetooth) and the check's own note lines, so a future reader hits the warning at the same moment they hit the number. senechal b85640c.

RELATED, same session, possibly the same pattern's sibling and offered as a second data point rather than a second ask: senechal's own remedy script printed 'written.' and exited 0 while both of its file writes had failed. Caught only by testing the FAILURE path; the happy path passed cleanly. That is 'fails loud / no exit-0 no-ops' reproduced by the project that keeps finding it in others, which is now at least the second recorded instance of a remedy exhibiting the class it was written to prevent (see senechal .scheduler/FOCUS.md 2026-07-27 on 6d0c266 for the first).

Realisateur's call whether either belongs in BUILD-DISCIPLINE.md, whether the first is really just a special case of an existing pattern, and whether the phrasing above is right. senechal is not tracking this as an open finding -- it is a suggestion about doctrine, not a defect in the estate.
